  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday Night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1017 AM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nConfidence is growing that our next weather maker will be in here \nWednesday evening. The longwave pattern over the eastern half of the \nUS will allow for the first of (potentially) two lobes of energy \nto develop a surface low over the mid-Mississippi Wednesday \nafternoon. The question is, where will this sfc low track? \nYesterday, the GFS had the low near Memphis. Today, it's much \nfurther north and much more in line with the Euro solution. The \ncatch is, it's still not as far north as the Euro continues to \nshow it, so I have to imagine the GFS isn't done with its \nadjustments on this system. In the end, showers and a a few \nrumbles of thunder will become possible Wednesday evening for most\nof Middle TN. There is some shear with this system and mid-level \nlapse rates are healthy, but sfc instability is greatly lacking. \nFor this reason, I'm not concerned about severe weather at this \npoint.\n\nThe trajectory of this upper system is going to dictate what happens \nafter Wednesday evening. Because of the more southerly track the GFS \nhas, additional showers are being shown Thursday and Thursday night \nas a second lob of energy rounds the base of the longwave pattern. \nThe Euro does not have this, which is why NBM PoPs for the second \nhalf of the week aren't as high as they otherwise would be. So, \nwe'll have to see what the next couple days of model runs do with \nthis system. What is agreed upon is the additional shot of cold air \nbehind this system. Widespread 20s look increasingly possible for \nmorning lows next weekend.",
